      // [src_lks_britannica, src_lks_bloomberg_money, src_lks_encyclopedia | 1950s-2010s | reference biography + business-wire profile + company history] Britannica and Bloomberg trace a path from plastics and artificial flowers to property, then ports, telecommunications, retail, energy and technology stakes; the company history shows Hutchison's ports, telecommunications and utility diversification under him and the 1997 and 2015 restructurings reorganised the group to fit changing conditions. Consistent across independent sources; credit for individual units is shared with managers, so a good rather than extreme score.
      adaptability: [72, 0.6, "d", "A"],
      // [src_lks_bloomberg_money, src_lks_forbes, src_lks_encyclopedia, src_lks_britannica | 1940s-1960s | business-wire profile + magazine profile + company history + reference biography] Bloomberg, Forbes and Britannica agree that he left school as a refugee, worked long factory hours, started his own plastics firm with small savings and relatives' loans in 1950 and, when his factory lease could not be renewed, bought the site, which Bloomberg dates as the start of his property career. The origin story is documented by several outlets but is a widely retold narrative, so confidence is moderate.
      resourcefulness: [70, 0.58, "d", "A"],
      // [src_lks_bloomberg_money, src_lks_forbes, src_lks_encyclopedia | 1940s-2018 | business-wire profile + magazine profile + company history] Bloomberg reports teenage 16-hour working days and a career spanning more than half a century until his 2018 retirement at 89, Forbes notes he stayed on as adviser, and the company history shows him continuing to expand through setbacks such as the loss-making early telecom units. Long-run commitment is well documented, but no source describes specific episodes of effort under setback, so it is strong inference.
      persistence: [66, 0.52, "s", "A"],
      // [src_lks_bloomberg_money, src_lks_britannica, src_lks_encyclopedia | 1967-2000s | business-wire profile + reference biography + company history] Bloomberg describes buying property during the 1967 downturn; Britannica says Hutchison first invested in a money-losing UK phone operation before launching Orange; the company history notes start-up European 3G losses as an expected cost and a buyout of a profitable hotel lease to redevelop the land. Willingness to take large calculated positions is documented, but these were also hedged by selling at peaks, and no source gives his own risk reasoning independently, so confidence is moderate.
      risk_tolerance: [64, 0.5, "s", "D"],
      // [src_lks_bloomberg_money, src_lks_encyclopedia, src_lks_hkfp | 1997-2018 | business-wire profile + company history + news-agency report] Bloomberg quotes academic Joseph Fan that he had prepared his son for succession for decades and the retirement was announced on a high note with strong results; the company history describes the March 1997 restructuring he engineered, and AFP reports the 2015 reorganisation and utilities merger as steps to improve future expansion. Structured, forward-looking corporate steps are documented; the motive for each is the companies' stated rationale plus an academic's reading, hence strong inference.
      planning_orientation: [64, 0.5, "s", "A"],
      // [src_lks_bloomberg_money, src_lks_encyclopedia, src_lks_rfa | 1967-2015 | business-wire profile + company history + critical news report] Bloomberg describes buying property in the 1967 downturn as others sold, and the company history and RFA record him selling assets when others expected expansion; Bloomberg and the RFA-reported editorial also note critics' view that part of his success rested on connections. Contrarian positioning is documented but its independence of mind is inferred from behaviour and partly contested, hence modest confidence.
      independent_thinking: [62, 0.48, "s", "D"],
      // [src_lks_forbes, src_lks_britannica, src_lks_stanford | 1980-2010s | magazine profile + reference biography + university communications] Forbes reports more than 3.8 billion dollars donated by his foundation, over 80 percent to Greater China; Britannica dates the foundation to 1980 and notes gifts to universities and hospitals; Stanford records a pledge of one-third of his assets and gifts of more than 1.45 billion dollars by 2010. The giving is documented, but the motive statements come from Li himself or the recipient's communications office, and critics frame his dominance differently, so this is a modest strong-inference row.
