      experimentation: [66, 0.5, "d", "A"],
      // [src_nureyev_stagenoise, src_nureyev_nation, src_nureyev_seattle | 1950s-1960s | reviews of Kavanagh's biography] Baum highlights his 'absolute hunger to learn, to embrace the new and to take risks'; The Nation reports his fascination with Danish style via film of Erik Bruhn and with Balanchine; Macdonald notes his fascination with Balanchine's work. Reviewers' summaries of one biography, so moderate confidence.
      curiosity: [70, 0.5, "d", "A"],
      // [src_nureyev_britannica, src_nureyev_kavanagh_fonteyn | 1960s-1980s | encyclopaedia + authorised-biography extract] Britannica records that he never joined a major Western company and instead worked with many companies on a temporary basis; the UXL entry counts over ninety roles and more than thirty companies; Kavanagh's Foundation extract describes him absorbing English schooling from Fonteyn and being transformed into 'an English Nureyev'. Consistent with adjusting style to different companies and traditions.
      adaptability: [72, 0.55, "d", "A"],
      // [src_nureyev_britannica, src_nureyev_ebsco, src_nureyev_nation | 1958-1980s | encyclopaedias + critical review] Britannica notes he preferred to work with companies on a temporary basis rather than join one; EBSCO says he was frequently in trouble for bending or ignoring company rules; The Nation reports the 1960 Kirov costume dispute. Consistent with a strong need to control his own working terms, which cost him goodwill with managers and colleagues.
      autonomy_need: [76, 0.55, "d", "D"],
      // [src_nureyev_kavanagh_fonteyn, src_nureyev_nation, src_nureyev_stagenoise | 1960s-1980s | authorised-biography extract + critical review + reviewer] Kavanagh's extract records rehearsal clashes with Fonteyn and Ashton's hurt at his abusive treatment of her; The Nation reports he made few friends as Paris Opera Ballet director and was sometimes brutal to colleagues; Baum cites rows with partners and the Royal Ballet's resentment. Documented repeatedly across reviewers, though all rely on one biography's material.
      conflict_tolerance: [74, 0.55, "d", "D"],
      // [src_nureyev_kavanagh_fonteyn, src_nureyev_ebsco, src_nureyev_nation, src_nureyev_stagenoise | 1962-1980s | mixed] The Fonteyn partnership is described as a constructive exchange of styles with mutual respect and EBSCO says critics felt each completed the other; against this, The Nation cites John Martin's complaint that he gave himself all the plums and his restagings centred himself, and Baum describes resentment inside the Royal Ballet when their billing eclipsed others. Strong partnership at the centre, ego-driven conduct toward the wider company.
